  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so we have zero knowledge of the performance floor for either modality. The most fundamental uncertainty is whether the image modality alone provides useful signal for this task, and at what level. Without a baseline, any multimodal result would be uninterpretable—we could not attribute performance to image, RNA, or fusion. 2) What this tests: config_019 (image_only, singleprofile, resnet50, no aggregation, no fusion) is the simplest possible image-only model. It isolates the image modality with a well-understood encoder (ResNet50), a single profile (no aggregation complexity), and no fusion step. This gives a clean reference performance for the image branch. 3) Why it is the best first experiment: Under a limited budget, the highest-value first experiment is one that establishes a performance anchor with minimal confounding variables. This single run lets every subsequent experiment be interpreted as a delta: does adding RNA help (compare to rna_only), does multiprofile help (compare to multiprofile image_only), does a stronger image encoder help (compare to ViT-L), does fusion strategy matter (compare concat vs. weighted multimodal). Starting with the simplest, most interpretable baseline maximizes the information gained per experiment and avoids the risk of over- or under-attributing performance to a complex multimodal configuration before we know what each component contributes."
}

